        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(tls): bound self-signed certificate generation",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nWhen Gateway TLS is enabled with automatic self-signed certificate generation, Gateway startup invokes `openssl req`. If OpenSSL stalls, startup can wait indefinitely. Adding a deadline alone is not enough: interruption while OpenSSL writes directly to the configured paths can leave a partial key or certificate that blocks every later startup.\n\nThis is an existing gap, not a regression from #106495. Before that refactor, the path used Node.js `execFile` with its default zero timeout. #106495 moved it to `runExec`, but the call still had no `timeoutMs`.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThe Gateway TLS owner now:\n\n- bounds OpenSSL generation at 30 seconds through the existing `runExec` lifecycle;\n- generates the key and certificate only inside private sibling staging directories;\n- applies private modes and validates the staged key/certificate pair before publication;\n- publishes with no-overwrite hard links, rolling back only outputs whose file identity still belongs to this attempt;\n- removes staging material on success and ordinary failure.\n\nOpenSSL therefore never receives the configured final paths. A timeout or generation failure cannot strand a partial final pair, while a file created concurrently at a final path is not overwritten. There is no new config, environment variable, dependency, or migration.\n\nThe dependency contracts were checked directly against OpenClaw's `runExec`/Execa 9.6.1 timeout lifecycle, `@openclaw/fs-safe` 0.4.1 file-identity helper, and the official `openssl req` behavior.\n\n## User Impact\n\nGateway startup no longer hangs indefinitely during automatic certificate generation. At the deadline it returns the existing TLS-required-but-disabled error state with no partial final artifacts; a subsequent start can generate a usable pair normally.\n\n## Evidence\n\nExact head: `5a60a5c86e6e8bcf9f65234162781cc871e330dc`, based on `d54da31541762da342d3b8e262bb53e6119016cd`. The original timeout commit is range-diff identical after rebase.\n\nReal production-path proof used Node 24.18.0 on Linux/x64 and the actual `loadGatewayTlsRuntime` plus `runExec` lifecycle:\n\n- a controlled OpenSSL wrapper wrote staged material and then stalled;\n- the first call returned after 30,042 ms with `enabled=false` and the timeout error;\n- configured final entries after timeout: none;\n- surviving OpenSSL processes after timeout: 0;\n- a second call on the same configured paths completed in 401 ms with `enabled=true`;\n- final entries were exactly `gateway-cert.pem` and `gateway-key.pem`; private-key mode was `0600`.\n\nVerification on the exact head:\n\n- Node 24.18.0 focused test: `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/infra/tls/gateway.test.ts --run` - 11/11 passed.\n- Type-aware targeted oxlint - 0 warnings, 0 errors.\n- Targeted `oxfmt --check` and `git diff --check origin/main...HEAD` - passed.\n- Fresh independent Codex adversarial review inspected the whole module, callers, tests, sibling behavior, `runExec`/Execa and fs-safe contracts; no actionable finding, estimated merge probability 87-90%.\n\nThe structured autoreview helper rejected the repository's pre-existing PEM test fixture before reaching its review engine. No bypass was used; the independent Codex review above is the documented fallback.\n",
        "branch": "alix/gateway-tls-generation-timeout",
